  "question": {
    "text": "<p>In the figure, a very large plane sheet of positive charge is shown. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are two points at distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>l</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>l</mi></math> from the charge distribution. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#963;</mi></math> is the surface charge density, then the magnitude of electric fields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> respectively are&nbsp;</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0124_d2d35fba8904.png\" style=\"width: 230px; height: 160px;\" /></p><p>&nbsp;</p>"
  },
